From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:24:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: keep offloading half-closed TCP connections

nf_flow_state_check() sets NF_FLOW_CLOSING on the first FIN or RST that
the software path sees in either direction. The GC then removes the
hardware entry of a CLOSING flow, and flow_offload_refresh() never
offers a CLOSING flow to the hardware again, so the flow stays in
software until it ends.

A FIN only ends the direction of its sender, though. A connection whose
one end shuts down its sending side early and keeps receiving, such as a
client after its request or a receiver that has nothing to send, carries
the rest of its transfer after that FIN, and all of it goes through the
software path. The software path does see such a FIN. The flow is
created when the handshake completes, and a receiver that has nothing to
send can close its side right after that, before the hardware forwards
any packet of the flow. A later FIN reaches it as well where the
hardware follows the rule offered to it, which matches only TCP packets
without FIN and RST, or passes the TCP control packets of its entries to
the CPU on its own.

Record the FIN of each direction in a flag of its own,
NF_FLOW_FIN_ORIGINAL and NF_FLOW_FIN_REPLY, and set NF_FLOW_CLOSING only
on an RST or once both directions have sent a FIN. Both callers pass the
direction of the packet for that. A half-closed connection then keeps
its hardware entry, and refresh may offer it again, until the other end
closes as well or one end resets the connection. From then on the flow
is handled as before.

The FINs of the two directions can be handled on two CPUs at the same
time. Each CPU sets the flag of its direction with test_and_set_bit()
and only then tests the flag of the other direction. Both flags are bits
of flow->flags, and atomic operations on one word take effect one after
the other: the later of the two test_and_set_bit() calls finds the flag
of the earlier one already set, and the test that follows on the same
CPU cannot read an older value of the word. test_and_set_bit() is also
fully ordered. At least the CPU that sets the second flag thus sees both
and sets NF_FLOW_CLOSING. A repeated FIN finds its flag set and changes
nothing.

This holds only while no write undoes a FIN flag. flow_offload_add()
sets NF_FLOW_HW with __set_bit() after it has published the flow, a
plain read-modify-write of the word that can undo a FIN flag set on
another CPU at the same moment, and the flow would then not become
CLOSING when the other end closes. Use set_bit() there; all other
writes to flow->flags after the flow is published are atomic already.

Before this change NF_FLOW_CLOSING meant that a FIN or an RST had been
seen, and the SYN check and flow_offload_fixup_ct() keep that meaning by
testing the FIN flags as well. A SYN that reaches the software path
still tears down a flow that has seen a FIN or an RST, and a torn down
flow that has seen a FIN still moves its conntrack entry to
TCP_CONNTRACK_CLOSE with the CLOSE timeout. Only the GC and refresh,
which end the hardware offload of a CLOSING flow, treat a half-closed
flow differently: they let it keep its hardware entry. A flowtable
without hardware offload therefore behaves as before, and so do
protocols other than TCP and act_ct, which tears a flow down on its own
at the first FIN or RST.

Where only one FIN reaches nf_flow_state_check(), because the hardware
forwards the other or the other takes the slow path, the flow keeps its
hardware entry, like a flow whose FINs the software path never saw,
until it expires or an RST ends it, and its conntrack entry is still
fixed up as closing. Hardware that forwards SYN packets, which the rule
does not exclude, likewise forwards a SYN that matches the entry of a
half-closed flow, as it does for an open one, without the SYN check
seeing it.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
diff -urNp a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
--- a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
+++ b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
@@ -174,6 +174,8 @@ enum nf_flow_flags {
 	NF_FLOW_HW_PENDING,
 	NF_FLOW_HW_BIDIRECTIONAL,
 	NF_FLOW_HW_ESTABLISHED,
+	NF_FLOW_FIN_ORIGINAL,
+	NF_FLOW_FIN_REPLY,
 };
 
 enum flow_offload_type {
diff -urNp a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -206,7 +206,9 @@ static void flow_offload_fixup_ct(struct
 		 * allows TCP reopen from conntrack. Otherwise, pick up from
 		 * the last seen TCP state.
 		 */
-		closing = test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags);
+		closing = test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags) ||
+			  test_bit(NF_FLOW_FIN_ORIGINAL, &flow->flags) ||
+			  test_bit(NF_FLOW_FIN_REPLY, &flow->flags);
 		if (closing) {
 			flow_offload_fixup_tcp(ct, TCP_CONNTRACK_CLOSE);
 			timeout = READ_ONCE(tn->timeouts[TCP_CONNTRACK_CLOSE]);
@@ -349,7 +351,7 @@ int flow_offload_add(struct nf_flowtable
 	nf_ct_refresh(flow->ct, NF_CT_DAY);
 
 	if (nf_flowtable_hw_offload(flow_table)) {
-		__set_bit(NF_FLOW_HW, &flow->flags);
+		set_bit(NF_FLOW_HW, &flow->flags);
 		nf_flow_offload_add(flow_table, flow);
 	}
 
diff -urNp a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
@@ -20,7 +20,8 @@
 #include <linux/udp.h>
 
 static int nf_flow_state_check(struct flow_offload *flow, int proto,
-			       struct sk_buff *skb, unsigned int thoff)
+			       struct sk_buff *skb, unsigned int thoff,
+			       enum flow_offload_tuple_dir dir)
 {
 	struct tcphdr *tcph;
 
@@ -28,13 +29,26 @@ static int nf_flow_state_check(struct fl
 		return 0;
 
 	tcph = (void *)(skb_network_header(skb) + thoff);
-	if (tcph->syn && test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags)) {
+	if (tcph->syn && (test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags) ||
+			  test_bit(NF_FLOW_FIN_ORIGINAL, &flow->flags) ||
+			  test_bit(NF_FLOW_FIN_REPLY, &flow->flags))) {
 		flow_offload_teardown(flow);
 		return -1;
 	}
 
-	if ((tcph->fin || tcph->rst) &&
-	    !test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags))
+	if (tcph->fin) {
+		enum nf_flow_flags fin = NF_FLOW_FIN_ORIGINAL;
+		enum nf_flow_flags peer_fin = NF_FLOW_FIN_REPLY;
+
+		if (dir == FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_REPLY)
+			swap(fin, peer_fin);
+
+		if (!test_and_set_bit(fin, &flow->flags) &&
+		    test_bit(peer_fin, &flow->flags))
+			set_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags);
+	}
+
+	if (tcph->rst && !test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags))
 		set_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -415,7 +429,7 @@ static int nf_flow_offload_forward(struc
 
 	iph = (struct iphdr *)(skb_network_header(skb) + ctx->offset);
 	thoff = (iph->ihl * 4) + ctx->offset;
-	if (nf_flow_state_check(flow, iph->protocol, skb, thoff))
+	if (nf_flow_state_check(flow, iph->protocol, skb, thoff, dir))
 		return 0;
 
 	if (!nf_flow_dst_check(&tuplehash->tuple)) {
@@ -712,7 +726,7 @@ static int nf_flow_offload_ipv6_forward(
 
 	ip6h = (struct ipv6hdr *)(skb_network_header(skb) + ctx->offset);
 	thoff = sizeof(*ip6h) + ctx->offset;
-	if (nf_flow_state_check(flow, ip6h->nexthdr, skb, thoff))
+	if (nf_flow_state_check(flow, ip6h->nexthdr, skb, thoff, dir))
 		return 0;
 
 	if (!nf_flow_dst_check(&tuplehash->tuple)) {
